Comment (cmd/seed/workflows.go:186):
The workflow definition key is trimmed into the local `key` variable for empty/duplicate checks, but the trimmed value is not propagated back onto `seedDef.Key` before generating deterministic UUIDs and running the transaction. If a seed file contains leading/trailing whitespace in `key`, the importer will treat it as equivalent for duplicate detection but will still generate IDs from the untrimmed key, breaking idempotency (and potentially creating different rows if the whitespace is later corrected). Normalize `seedDef.Key` to the trimmed value before proceeding so all downstream logic uses the same canonical key.
Implementation guidance: In cmd/seed/workflows.go, update importWorkflowSeeds so the trimmed workflow definition key is propagated before the transaction calls importWorkflowSeedDefinition. After key := strings.TrimSpace(seedDef.Key) passes the empty and duplicate checks, assign seedDef.Key = key before seenKeys[key] is recorded or at least before db.WithContext(ctx).Transaction is invoked. This ensures deterministicWorkflowSeedUUID("workflow-definition", seedDef.Key) and all nested deterministic IDs use the same canonical key that duplicate detection uses. Also update the failure log in that loop to report the canonical key after normalization. Add or update a focused test in cmd/seed/workflows_test.go that imports a seed definition with leading/trailing whitespace in Key, verifies the stored workflow definition key is trimmed, and verifies re-importing the same logical key without whitespace updates the same row rather than creating a second workflow definition. Do not change the deterministic UUID seed format or the v1 suffix.
